::ALG2_U03_L07_GP01::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L07_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6
Question:
What is the vertical asymptote of f(x)\=1/(x-4)?
{
=A. x \= 4#Correct. The denominator is zero when x - 4 \= 0.
~B. x \= -4#The sign is reversed.
~C. y \= 4#A vertical asymptote is written x \= value.
~D. x \= 0#The denominator is not zero at 0.
}

::ALG2_U03_L07_GP02::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L07_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6
Question:
What is the domain of f(x)\=5/(x+2)?
{
~A. x !\= 2#The sign is reversed.
=B. x !\= -2#Correct. The denominator x + 2 cannot equal 0.
~C. all real numbers#x \= -2 is not allowed.
~D. x >\= -2#This is a radical-style domain, not rational.
}

::ALG2_U03_L07_GP03::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L07_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6
Question:
What is the horizontal asymptote of f(x)\=(3x+1)/(x-5)?
{
~A. x \= 5#That is the vertical asymptote.
~B. y \= 1#This uses the constant, not leading coefficients.
=C. y \= 3#Correct. The degrees match, so use the ratio of leading coefficients 3/1.
~D. y \= 0#Equal degrees do not give y \= 0 here.
}

::ALG2_U03_L07_GP04::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L07_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6
Question:
In T(n)\=120/n, why is n\=0 not allowed?
{
~A. the time would be 120#120/0 is undefined.
~B. the model becomes linear#The issue is division by zero.
~C. n\=0 is always the maximum#It is not in the domain.
=D. zero workers is not meaningful and division by zero is undefined#Correct. The denominator cannot be zero, and the context also rejects zero workers.
}

::ALG2_U03_L07_GP05::
Question ID: ALG2_U03_L07_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.RR.5; MLA.A2.RR.6
Question:
For f(x)\=2/(x+7), what line does the graph approach near the restriction?
{
=A. x \= -7#Correct. The denominator is zero at x \= -7, creating a vertical asymptote.
~B. x \= 7#The sign is reversed.
~C. y \= -7#A vertical asymptote uses x \= value.
~D. y \= 2#This is not the restriction line.
}

